WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.187 --> 00:00:05.007
[MUSIC]

00:00:05.007 --> 00:00:07.734
So welcome for joining us.

00:00:07.734 --> 00:00:09.809
We're really excited to have you here.

00:00:09.809 --> 00:00:15.443
I want to let everyone know that Renee is
now in her second week of fun employment,

00:00:15.443 --> 00:00:16.976
as we like to call it.

00:00:16.976 --> 00:00:21.430
So we just want to welcome you to that
life stage we know a lot about it.

00:00:21.430 --> 00:00:23.381
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Im retired.

00:00:23.381 --> 00:00:26.831
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Temporarily.

00:00:26.831 --> 00:00:32.073
&gt;&gt; Starting there, I'm just curious,
after 30 years at Intel,

00:00:32.073 --> 00:00:38.096
what was it like the morning you woke up
and you knew, you were going to leave?

00:00:38.096 --> 00:00:39.634
&gt;&gt; Yeah, that's a great question.

00:00:39.634 --> 00:00:42.494
&gt;&gt; I don't know that it was one morning,
and

00:00:42.494 --> 00:00:46.510
I apologize, I don't want to
have my back to these people.

00:00:46.510 --> 00:00:49.610
I think it was a journey
of a couple of things.

00:00:49.610 --> 00:00:55.030
I think everyone in the audience probably
knows that I was a candidate to be CEO and

00:00:55.030 --> 00:00:59.240
I was not selected for
co-CEO which was the proposal we made,

00:00:59.240 --> 00:01:01.240
but to become president.

00:01:01.240 --> 00:01:03.010
So I think that was the first.

00:01:04.105 --> 00:01:06.765
I actually, at the time, was thrilled.

00:01:06.765 --> 00:01:09.585
And I still love the company so much.

00:01:09.585 --> 00:01:12.355
I was so
excited to serve in that capacity.

00:01:12.355 --> 00:01:16.817
But what I didn't realize three
years ago is that I really

00:01:16.817 --> 00:01:21.117
did want to be the leader
of my own company.

00:01:21.117 --> 00:01:25.867
And that became more clear to me through
the journey of the two and a half

00:01:25.867 --> 00:01:31.077
years up to the point I decided to resign,
that I really wanted to be in charge.

00:01:31.077 --> 00:01:35.347
And it turns out, you kind of get to this
point in your career where it's like, and

00:01:35.347 --> 00:01:37.287
being in charge isn't so
much about being the boss.

00:01:37.287 --> 00:01:40.071
It's more about setting the strategy and

00:01:40.071 --> 00:01:45.320
creating the leadership environment
that you want, and making the decisions

00:01:45.320 --> 00:01:50.748
around how you're going to handle things
that are more difficult in a certain way.

00:01:50.748 --> 00:01:55.556
And so I kind of, I think it was kind
of in the background, but there were

00:01:55.556 --> 00:02:00.868
a series of events that occurred where
I would've made different decisions.

00:02:00.868 --> 00:02:04.622
And I realized at that point
that it as time for me to go.

00:02:04.622 --> 00:02:08.514
Because I had a different point of
view about what was possible for

00:02:08.514 --> 00:02:11.790
the company and
the path that I would take to get there.

00:02:11.790 --> 00:02:15.804
And I think everybody knows I worked for
Andy forever and

00:02:15.804 --> 00:02:20.844
that he's still my mentor and
he would say, if you ever get to the point

00:02:20.844 --> 00:02:25.557
where you no longer agree with
the strategy, what are you doing?

00:02:27.507 --> 00:02:28.713
And so I said, what am I doing?

00:02:28.713 --> 00:02:31.327
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And then I quit.

00:02:31.327 --> 00:02:32.956
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:02:32.956 --> 00:02:34.595
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] And everyone says to me,

00:02:34.595 --> 00:02:38.831
that was either the bravest thing that
anyone's ever done, or the stupidest.

00:02:38.831 --> 00:02:41.347
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; We're still going to figure that

00:02:41.347 --> 00:02:42.900
part out.

00:02:42.900 --> 00:02:48.125
And you know what, I did my exit
interview last Monday morning.

00:02:48.125 --> 00:02:52.375
And the head of human resources who's
worked for me for, I don't know,

00:02:52.375 --> 00:02:54.270
18 years, had to do the exit.

00:02:54.270 --> 00:02:58.404
And I told him in advance, six months ago,
I said, Matt, you're going to need to get

00:02:58.404 --> 00:03:01.889
ready for this, this is going to be
very emotional for you, he's like,

00:03:01.889 --> 00:03:03.390
we're going to talk about you.

00:03:03.390 --> 00:03:06.120
I said, no we're not, because I'm pretty
sure I'm going to be okay in that meeting,

00:03:06.120 --> 00:03:07.320
it's going to be you.

00:03:07.320 --> 00:03:08.920
And it went exactly the way I thought.

00:03:08.920 --> 00:03:11.227
They all came in, and they were all upset.

00:03:11.227 --> 00:03:13.210
Are you still going to
take our phone calls?

00:03:13.210 --> 00:03:15.768
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And I'm like geez, okay,

00:03:15.768 --> 00:03:18.128
I've gotta go because I'm retired now.

00:03:18.128 --> 00:03:22.875
[LAUGH] Yeah, it was hard.

00:03:22.875 --> 00:03:23.807
It's still hard, right?

00:03:23.807 --> 00:03:25.023
I love Intel.

00:03:25.023 --> 00:03:29.451
And I think Intel has
incredible possibilities,

00:03:29.451 --> 00:03:32.830
but I would take a different journey.

00:03:34.100 --> 00:03:35.450
&gt;&gt; So, let's talk about that.

00:03:35.450 --> 00:03:39.924
You mentioned having a difference of
opinion on the strategy for intel.

00:03:39.924 --> 00:03:42.654
In case the audience doesn't know,

00:03:42.654 --> 00:03:46.540
a few instances in your career,
this came up prior.

00:03:46.540 --> 00:03:50.677
So you had the early idea of Intel
housing outsource servers for

00:03:50.677 --> 00:03:54.982
small businesses well before Amazon and
others were doing that.

00:03:54.982 --> 00:03:58.795
The bust of 2001 sort of led them
to move away from that strategy.

00:03:58.795 --> 00:04:03.261
Later on you tried to guide Intel
towards to be an early adopter of

00:04:03.261 --> 00:04:04.843
the mobile strategy.

00:04:04.843 --> 00:04:09.588
That again,
was an initiative that was not successful.

00:04:09.588 --> 00:04:14.320
Talk to us about what it's
like to have that vision, and

00:04:14.320 --> 00:04:18.660
some of the difficulties of shifting a
behemoth like Intel towards achieving it.

00:04:18.660 --> 00:04:22.580
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I think the first,
they're kind of two different situations.

00:04:22.580 --> 00:04:27.640
The first one was a kind of
an acknowledgement about how people would

00:04:27.640 --> 00:04:32.830
want to consume technology over
the horizon, and as Andy says to me, well

00:04:32.830 --> 00:04:36.135
you were absolutely 100% directionally
correct just a decade too soon.

00:04:36.135 --> 00:04:38.638
[LAUGH] Which is the same as being wrong.

00:04:38.638 --> 00:04:39.240
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So

00:04:39.240 --> 00:04:43.440
that was his polite way
of saying you blew it.

00:04:43.440 --> 00:04:46.733
Although he, by the way,
was the one who, he and Craig,

00:04:46.733 --> 00:04:49.351
gave us way too much money for
that endeavor.

00:04:49.351 --> 00:04:50.896
So that was a different situation.

00:04:50.896 --> 00:04:55.025
That was a situation of
making what I would call,

00:04:55.025 --> 00:04:59.750
managerial, or
more junior managerial mistakes.

00:04:59.750 --> 00:05:02.250
It was my first senior management job.

00:05:02.250 --> 00:05:05.550
I wrote a business plan that asked for
a lot of money.

00:05:05.550 --> 00:05:07.740
$100 million is a lot of money.

00:05:07.740 --> 00:05:09.490
They gave me 350 million, okay?

00:05:09.490 --> 00:05:13.247
So when somebody gives you way
more money than you've asked for,

00:05:13.247 --> 00:05:15.443
what's the first thing that you say?

00:05:15.443 --> 00:05:17.920
You're in this graduate
school business at Stanford.

00:05:17.920 --> 00:05:18.585
So let's hear it.

00:05:18.585 --> 00:05:20.850
&gt;&gt; [INAUDIBLE]
&gt;&gt; No thank you!

00:05:20.850 --> 00:05:22.060
No thank you,

00:05:22.060 --> 00:05:26.230
I don't need that much money because
if you take it, they want a return.

00:05:26.230 --> 00:05:29.430
So you don't want to take more
money than your business plan,

00:05:29.430 --> 00:05:33.000
until you actually know or
hit the milestone at which you need it.

00:05:33.000 --> 00:05:35.600
So when people want to over finance
you because they think you're a little

00:05:35.600 --> 00:05:36.340
unicorn.

00:05:36.340 --> 00:05:39.015
You say no thank you, I'll be back.

00:05:39.015 --> 00:05:40.582
And after I prove to you
I've made this milestone.

00:05:40.582 --> 00:05:42.086
Okay, that's your little tip of the day.

00:05:42.086 --> 00:05:42.622
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So

00:05:42.622 --> 00:05:46.810
I've of course said, my gosh $350 million,
that's a lot of money.

00:05:46.810 --> 00:05:48.510
Think of all the things I could build.

00:05:48.510 --> 00:05:49.048
Which I did.
And

00:05:49.048 --> 00:05:51.483
then I got to lay all those people off.

00:05:51.483 --> 00:05:55.955
So I think that was a learning
experience on two sides.

00:05:55.955 --> 00:06:00.862
One, I didn't know enough to stick to my
plan and on the other side of it, I wasn't

00:06:00.862 --> 00:06:05.415
a senior enough manager to convince the
then CEO that we should stick it out in

00:06:05.415 --> 00:06:10.410
a smaller capacity, like downscale and
then stick it out through the down turn.

00:06:10.410 --> 00:06:16.410
He just lost faith and decided to
sell it off, so that's Teradata.

00:06:16.410 --> 00:06:20.150
But the second one is
a completely different situation.

00:06:20.150 --> 00:06:23.672
This is a situation where I was
a senior manager at the time.

00:06:23.672 --> 00:06:25.547
I was Executive Vice President
of the company.

00:06:25.547 --> 00:06:27.915
And the CEO and I disagreed.

00:06:27.915 --> 00:06:30.532
And he's the boss.

00:06:30.532 --> 00:06:36.200
And I think that I did the right thing for

00:06:36.200 --> 00:06:40.710
our shareholders and I think it was
the right strategy for the company, and

00:06:40.710 --> 00:06:43.240
I think we'd be a very different company
if we had been able to pull it off.

00:06:44.260 --> 00:06:49.590
And Brian and I tried, but it was four
years, or maybe five, Robert, right?

00:06:49.590 --> 00:06:52.060
Robert's doing a case about it later.

00:06:52.060 --> 00:06:53.990
There's a case about this.

00:06:53.990 --> 00:06:57.810
And we lost too much momentum,

00:06:59.227 --> 00:07:03.550
had 2 billion units in the field and was
shipping almost 2 billion a year now new.

00:07:03.550 --> 00:07:10.150
And at certain point, in my industry,
in any industry, when you have a platform.

00:07:10.150 --> 00:07:12.920
Once you get a network effect going,
it's just too late and

00:07:12.920 --> 00:07:14.840
I think that was the case.

00:07:14.840 --> 00:07:19.497
But that was a case where you had to
decide what you believed, and you had to

00:07:19.497 --> 00:07:24.776
stand up for yourself at risk of political
risk and I think that's very dangerous.

00:07:24.776 --> 00:07:26.482
And I think you have to
know what's at play.

00:07:26.482 --> 00:07:28.480
And so I of course did what I always do.

00:07:28.480 --> 00:07:31.680
I went and talked to people who've
advised me through my career.

00:07:31.680 --> 00:07:32.970
You need mentors.

00:07:32.970 --> 00:07:36.330
This mentor thing is real,
you really do need mentors and

00:07:36.330 --> 00:07:41.200
I remember that Andy said to me,
this is one of those

00:07:41.200 --> 00:07:43.700
situations where if it doesn't go well,
you might have to leave.

00:07:45.360 --> 00:07:48.151
And I say yeah, and he said but
you should do what you believe,

00:07:48.151 --> 00:07:49.776
if you think you're really right.

00:07:49.776 --> 00:07:50.956
Okay, that's not helpful.

00:07:50.956 --> 00:07:52.900
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Okay, now what?

00:07:52.900 --> 00:07:55.384
But, I knew mobile was right and
I believed it.

00:07:55.384 --> 00:07:57.195
I was on the board of Vodafone.

00:07:57.195 --> 00:07:58.774
I could see the data.

00:07:58.774 --> 00:08:02.582
I understood Paul's position.

00:08:02.582 --> 00:08:07.548
We had a franchise that generates
cash that people dream about, and

00:08:07.548 --> 00:08:09.987
you don't want to disrupt that and

00:08:09.987 --> 00:08:15.560
pricing coming in below that is
a huge compression to your pricing.

00:08:15.560 --> 00:08:20.630
But it's also was consistent with
the philosophy of the company which was

00:08:20.630 --> 00:08:25.350
to always be a first mover and embracing
that which is going to disrupt you.

00:08:25.350 --> 00:08:28.440
Right, so
it was a typical Christianson situation.

00:08:28.440 --> 00:08:31.890
Seemed pretty strategically
straightforward and

00:08:31.890 --> 00:08:32.900
we couldn't execute it.

00:08:32.900 --> 00:08:34.720
And the why couldn't we execute it, why?

00:08:35.760 --> 00:08:41.000
Because people do what they think

00:08:41.000 --> 00:08:45.199
the CEO wants even if
they know it's wrong.

00:08:46.200 --> 00:08:47.760
That's what I learned.

00:08:47.760 --> 00:08:52.880
And that's a very dangerous phenomena
that people want to please and a lot

00:08:52.880 --> 00:08:57.880
of large companies, although I see it in
start ups too, are very hierarchical.

00:08:57.880 --> 00:09:00.610
And so they're like, hey,
this is what the boss wants.

00:09:00.610 --> 00:09:05.030
And we hear you Renee and we know you're
right, and we want to do that and

00:09:05.030 --> 00:09:07.680
we would do it, and we would start
with our hearts sworn in it.

00:09:07.680 --> 00:09:10.870
And then when we have resource allocate,
it all comes down to money, right, or

00:09:10.870 --> 00:09:12.710
resources, people, money.

00:09:12.710 --> 00:09:15.110
Whenever resource allocation conflict,

00:09:15.110 --> 00:09:17.510
it always goes the other way because
you know that's what the boss wants.

00:09:17.510 --> 00:09:18.010
&gt;&gt; Yeah, yeah.

00:09:19.340 --> 00:09:22.510
So you mentioned Andy and
you mentioned mentorship.

00:09:22.510 --> 00:09:25.250
You started in till 28 years ago-
&gt;&gt; I did.

00:09:25.250 --> 00:09:27.300
&gt;&gt; As a [LAUGH] technical-
&gt;&gt; But I was a lot younger.

00:09:27.300 --> 00:09:31.900
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] As a technical
assistant to Andy Grove,

00:09:31.900 --> 00:09:34.440
who is sort of a Silicon Valley great.

00:09:34.440 --> 00:09:38.860
&gt;&gt; That wasn't my first job, but it was
right at the seven year mark on my career.

00:09:38.860 --> 00:09:39.420
&gt;&gt; Okay.
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:09:39.420 --> 00:09:43.480
&gt;&gt; And so he was famous for
his confrontational nature,

00:09:43.480 --> 00:09:46.050
he like to call it
constructive confrontation.

00:09:47.100 --> 00:09:49.570
What did you learn while you were
working so closely with him and

00:09:49.570 --> 00:09:53.650
how did that inform how you decided to
manage when you got to the president

00:09:53.650 --> 00:09:57.000
role and throughout the rest
of your career with Intel.

00:09:57.000 --> 00:10:00.660
&gt;&gt; Well, I think Andy had a couple
of pieces of advice that he

00:10:00.660 --> 00:10:05.331
didn't always follow, but
that were actually very, very important.

00:10:05.331 --> 00:10:09.099
I mean, we don't always,
all aren't self-consistent, but

00:10:09.099 --> 00:10:13.480
one of them was to watch what people do,
not what they say.

00:10:13.480 --> 00:10:14.740
And he used to say,

00:10:14.740 --> 00:10:20.080
we'd be negotiating a partnership or
I'm an employer giving a presentation and

00:10:20.080 --> 00:10:22.715
it say, yeah, you hear what they
saying but what are they really doing.

00:10:22.715 --> 00:10:26.812
because whatever people are really doing
that's actually their strategy, right.

00:10:26.812 --> 00:10:28.250
Not what they're saying.

00:10:28.250 --> 00:10:34.734
So I have used that technique, I can't,
I mean probably almost every day,right?

00:10:34.734 --> 00:10:38.340
I read what people are going to say
to me and then I kind of figure out

00:10:38.340 --> 00:10:42.096
what have they actually been doing,
what's their real action?

00:10:42.096 --> 00:10:45.785
If they are consistent then you have
one point of view and if they're not,

00:10:45.785 --> 00:10:46.449
you don't.

00:10:46.449 --> 00:10:48.288
The other one is clarity imperative.

00:10:48.288 --> 00:10:51.352
And this is really important.

00:10:51.352 --> 00:10:58.202
[LAUGH] Andy used to be ruthless and
one of my first, I love this story.

00:10:58.202 --> 00:11:01.850
So I'm, all of, I don't know,
28 years old or something, and

00:11:01.850 --> 00:11:05.060
I go into this meeting and
the guy comes in and he's got all this and

00:11:05.060 --> 00:11:07.260
he's going to pitched for
a new networking business.

00:11:07.260 --> 00:11:10.620
We were in and out of networking
probably four times at Intel.

00:11:10.620 --> 00:11:15.990
And he begins pitching but he's actually
talking about something different.

00:11:15.990 --> 00:11:19.190
[LAUGH] And he's like that's
not the topic of this meeting.

00:11:19.190 --> 00:11:20.230
And he's like, well, yeah, no.

00:11:20.230 --> 00:11:23.352
Yeah, [LAUGH] I've told this story
before in one of your classes and

00:11:23.352 --> 00:11:26.880
he [LAUGH] goes, yeah, no,
that's not what we're here to discuss.

00:11:26.880 --> 00:11:30.280
You're not being clear, you don't
have your act together and he said,

00:11:30.280 --> 00:11:31.550
meeting is over, he got up and walked out.

00:11:32.650 --> 00:11:35.922
It was a room full of people,
I'm like, that's kind of cool.

00:11:35.922 --> 00:11:40.960
So but the clarity imperative
is really knowing what it

00:11:40.960 --> 00:11:46.300
is that matters and being able
to write it down in five slides.

00:11:46.300 --> 00:11:48.970
Like the five slide test
actually is hard and

00:11:48.970 --> 00:11:51.860
I think that's one of the things
that he really forced.

00:11:51.860 --> 00:11:56.140
He forced intellectual rigor and clarity
and that is something that has really been

00:11:56.140 --> 00:12:01.230
defining of my leadership
style because I will tend

00:12:01.230 --> 00:12:06.660
to find the thing that somebody's either
hiding or not saying and go after it.

00:12:06.660 --> 00:12:08.250
And that is so Andy.

00:12:08.250 --> 00:12:11.440
And I'll find it like in a millisecond,
it used to take me longer but now,

00:12:11.440 --> 00:12:12.011
not so much.

00:12:12.011 --> 00:12:13.442
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] And I'll go as soon as I find

00:12:13.442 --> 00:12:17.060
it, I know there's something there and
there's something they're not telling me.

00:12:17.060 --> 00:12:18.900
And there's a reason
they're not telling you.

00:12:18.900 --> 00:12:21.457
And whatever that is,
that's what's really going on.

00:12:23.691 --> 00:12:24.670
&gt;&gt; Very interesting.

00:12:24.670 --> 00:12:25.695
I hope you guys are writing this down.

00:12:25.695 --> 00:12:27.280
&gt;&gt; It just happened.

00:12:27.280 --> 00:12:29.290
I just told you the story
in the green room.

00:12:29.290 --> 00:12:32.970
I just joined the board of Citi, and the
CFO came in and he was talking about all

00:12:32.970 --> 00:12:37.060
these things, and I was like, wait, wait,
wait, wait, and he skipped over something.

00:12:37.060 --> 00:12:41.080
In the normal course of managing
corporate finance of a company of scale,

00:12:41.080 --> 00:12:43.230
Citi's about the same size as Intel.

00:12:43.230 --> 00:12:48.175
You would ask the following basic things,
and so of course I just asked the question

00:12:48.175 --> 00:12:52.852
he glossed over, I made him go back twice
and I knew there was something there.

00:12:52.852 --> 00:12:57.984
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] So okay, in terms of
talking about getting on boards and

00:12:57.984 --> 00:13:05.710
thinking about how you analyze what's
there versus maybe what's being unsaid.

00:13:05.710 --> 00:13:08.965
You have said that you wanted
to take on a CEO role somewhere?

00:13:08.965 --> 00:13:10.360
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:13:10.360 --> 00:13:14.060
&gt;&gt; What's your process for
deciding what exactly you're looking for?

00:13:15.090 --> 00:13:16.530
&gt;&gt; That is a great question.

00:13:17.700 --> 00:13:20.730
So my process isn't very good.

00:13:20.730 --> 00:13:24.490
And my mentor explained to me that
I really, really sucked at this.

00:13:24.490 --> 00:13:30.130
I made a list of the things I didn't
want to do, because [LAUGH] I decided

00:13:30.130 --> 00:13:33.860
that that I could do so many things,
and I was interested in so many things.

00:13:33.860 --> 00:13:36.900
And I really love my work at Vodafone,
for example, and

00:13:36.900 --> 00:13:39.580
that's completely different from
running a semiconductor company.

00:13:39.580 --> 00:13:43.181
And I find Citi very challenging and
Finn Tech and then what's going to happen,

00:13:43.181 --> 00:13:44.840
I'm really interested in that.

00:13:44.840 --> 00:13:45.840
So then I said, okay, but

00:13:45.840 --> 00:13:48.410
what are the things that really
I don't want to ever do again.

00:13:48.410 --> 00:13:53.250
The top of my list, my list right now
has three maybe four things on it.

00:13:53.250 --> 00:13:56.270
First, no bad boards,
because at the end of the day,

00:13:56.270 --> 00:13:58.870
when you're CEO, it's all about the board.

00:13:58.870 --> 00:14:02.817
If you have a dysfunctional board,
and a board that isn't supportive, or

00:14:02.817 --> 00:14:07.500
that has their own internal dynamic and
politics, life's too short for that.

00:14:07.500 --> 00:14:09.546
No bad boards, no bad bosses,

00:14:09.546 --> 00:14:15.158
well I actually don't really want a boss
ever again, [LAUGH] that's another topic.

00:14:15.158 --> 00:14:18.690
And I want to do something that matters.

00:14:18.690 --> 00:14:21.760
because there's lots of stuff that you can
do, there's lots of work in the world.

00:14:21.760 --> 00:14:25.280
But at this point for me, I want to do
something that really contributes and

00:14:25.280 --> 00:14:27.900
changes that matters.

00:14:27.900 --> 00:14:34.700
There are something big changes in front
of us that I think I could go work on.

00:14:34.700 --> 00:14:38.070
I have considered starting my own company.

00:14:38.070 --> 00:14:41.010
I'm a scale operator,
that's what I am, I know that.

00:14:41.010 --> 00:14:44.894
I've voluntarily advised start ups and
venture back,

00:14:44.894 --> 00:14:49.920
and I could give them advice but
I'm definitely a scale operator.

00:14:49.920 --> 00:14:53.610
So I've thought a lot
about starting my own, but

00:14:53.610 --> 00:14:58.388
that's 24/7, 365,
which I've done for 28 years.

00:14:58.388 --> 00:15:02.287
So I decided not to make
any of those decisions and

00:15:02.287 --> 00:15:06.293
to take a short break which
has been one week long.

00:15:06.293 --> 00:15:09.365
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] And so far, what do you think?

00:15:09.365 --> 00:15:10.483
&gt;&gt; I think I should get a job.

00:15:10.483 --> 00:15:14.713
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:15:14.713 --> 00:15:16.693
&gt;&gt; I woke up this morning, and I thought,

00:15:16.693 --> 00:15:18.740
thank God I have to go to Stanford today.

00:15:18.740 --> 00:15:20.009
At least I have a reason to get dressed.

00:15:20.009 --> 00:15:24.285
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So, I wake up at 5:30, and

00:15:24.285 --> 00:15:27.049
I read my mail, which now only
takes me about 15 minutes, and

00:15:27.049 --> 00:15:29.254
then I read all the news,
and now it's 6:15.

00:15:29.254 --> 00:15:31.860
Then I work out, and
I have elementary school children.

00:15:31.860 --> 00:15:33.120
Yeah, that's a whole another topic.

00:15:33.120 --> 00:15:36.306
So I can drive them to school,
which I find fascinating.

00:15:36.306 --> 00:15:41.415
And I also learned something important,
you can make any job stressful.

00:15:41.415 --> 00:15:45.315
Okay, so apparently driving
the carpool can be very stressful.

00:15:45.315 --> 00:15:47.845
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Not for me, but for others.

00:15:47.845 --> 00:15:50.927
[LAUGH] I'm like, seriously,
we're not solving here ladies,

00:15:50.927 --> 00:15:52.098
let's just move it along.

00:15:52.098 --> 00:15:55.334
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So how is your family factoring into

00:15:55.334 --> 00:15:58.549
your decision to
&gt;&gt; One of the reasons that I decided I

00:15:58.549 --> 00:16:03.106
should take at least more than a week off
is My older son is looking at colleges so

00:16:03.106 --> 00:16:05.332
we're doing the college tour thing.

00:16:05.332 --> 00:16:10.510
And I'm getting him ready to go off to
college or apply to college next year.

00:16:10.510 --> 00:16:14.070
So it's factoring in,
but my kids are worried.

00:16:14.070 --> 00:16:15.820
They're like, mom seriously?

00:16:15.820 --> 00:16:17.290
You're really high energy.

00:16:17.290 --> 00:16:17.998
We [NOISE].

00:16:17.998 --> 00:16:20.240
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; We don't want you around.

00:16:20.240 --> 00:16:22.870
So I have way too many boards.

00:16:22.870 --> 00:16:24.000
I have four boards right now.

00:16:24.000 --> 00:16:25.790
Everyone's like, are you going to
be a professional director?

00:16:25.790 --> 00:16:30.510
I'm like I want to say no I'm not, but
I think I am right now, but not forever,

00:16:30.510 --> 00:16:34.160
because I do want to stay active.

00:16:34.160 --> 00:16:41.050
And yeah, I'm just, most of the jobs
that are for CEOs, if you're not.

00:16:41.050 --> 00:16:44.400
I'm an entrepreneur, but
I'm a large scale entrepreneur.

00:16:44.400 --> 00:16:45.270
Are midcap.

00:16:45.270 --> 00:16:49.695
And midcap might not work for
me because like having one product and

00:16:49.695 --> 00:16:55.300
$600 billion of revenue is
like not my sweet spot, yeah.

00:16:55.300 --> 00:16:57.742
I'm more kind of in the 70 to 100 range.

00:16:57.742 --> 00:16:58.254
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:16:58.254 --> 00:17:00.770
&gt;&gt; Yeah because when I started looking at

00:17:00.770 --> 00:17:02.099
the balance sheet of Citi, right?

00:17:03.470 --> 00:17:06.740
Those numbers when they talk about
things we're talking about you know

00:17:06.740 --> 00:17:08.680
multiple trillions dollars made in a day.

00:17:08.680 --> 00:17:11.420
And you know the billion dollars
this way and that way and

00:17:11.420 --> 00:17:14.720
that's the kind of a scale of Intel
it's kind of a scale of Vodafone phone.

00:17:14.720 --> 00:17:16.210
It's kind of a scale of Oracle.

00:17:16.210 --> 00:17:18.880
So that's kind of the mentality
that I operate in and

00:17:18.880 --> 00:17:22.960
I think what happens Even if you shift
jobs a lot you should kind of develop

00:17:22.960 --> 00:17:26.810
skills in certain areas and
you have a certain kind of capability.

00:17:29.160 --> 00:17:33.540
&gt;&gt; Talking about scale on the level
that you're talking about scale, and

00:17:33.540 --> 00:17:36.510
then talking about being an entrepreneur,

00:17:36.510 --> 00:17:39.270
a lot of times those things
are very much at odds.

00:17:41.050 --> 00:17:45.650
Or actually, I think I have a quote
from you right here that says,

00:17:45.650 --> 00:17:47.840
we have a tolerance for risk taking.

00:17:47.840 --> 00:17:50.810
We have a tolerance for
taking measured risks in betting.

00:17:50.810 --> 00:17:52.540
It's not a tolerance for failure.

00:17:53.830 --> 00:17:56.770
As an entrepreneur,
how do you think about that?

00:17:56.770 --> 00:17:59.399
How do you sort of rectify and
what does that really mean?

00:18:00.610 --> 00:18:05.380
&gt;&gt; Well,
I think at the scale that we take risks,

00:18:05.380 --> 00:18:08.740
or I've taken risks, and I have failed.

00:18:08.740 --> 00:18:09.380
And we do fail.

00:18:09.380 --> 00:18:11.280
I mean, everybody who takes risks, fails.

00:18:11.280 --> 00:18:13.630
Failures part of taking risks, right?

00:18:13.630 --> 00:18:15.460
And you do have to have a tolerance for
failure,

00:18:15.460 --> 00:18:18.740
so that's probably not
really accurate that.

00:18:18.740 --> 00:18:20.265
I said that, I was wrong.

00:18:20.265 --> 00:18:23.340
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I mean you really have to be

00:18:23.340 --> 00:18:24.310
able to fail.

00:18:24.310 --> 00:18:29.070
You have to be measured though and I think
one of the things that I would say is that

00:18:29.070 --> 00:18:33.500
in a large entity you can
take bigger risks, but

00:18:33.500 --> 00:18:37.210
you have to ring fence.

00:18:37.210 --> 00:18:38.489
What's the downside?

00:18:38.489 --> 00:18:43.984
And so I do tend to understand like
how much are we going to really lose,

00:18:43.984 --> 00:18:47.072
how long could we really sustain this.

00:18:47.072 --> 00:18:52.301
You need to know those things because
large entities have very low tolerance for

00:18:52.301 --> 00:18:55.040
loss, very low tolerance in general.

00:18:55.040 --> 00:18:58.586
And so I think that's one of the things
that works against entrepreneurship.

00:18:58.586 --> 00:19:05.810
It's very unusual that you
end up in Google, right?

00:19:05.810 --> 00:19:06.410
Or even Oracle.

00:19:06.410 --> 00:19:10.480
I mean, they're transforming this
company to be a cloud company,

00:19:10.480 --> 00:19:15.190
at risk of a licensed business
that prints money, right?

00:19:15.190 --> 00:19:20.020
So, but that will make them a bigger and
greater company, and

00:19:20.020 --> 00:19:24.120
sustain them the next couple of decades,
so It's really one of these

00:19:24.120 --> 00:19:28.705
things where you have to
understand what your tolerance is.

00:19:28.705 --> 00:19:30.370
&gt;&gt; Mm-hm.
&gt;&gt; And large companies just don't have

00:19:30.370 --> 00:19:31.280
that much tolerance.

00:19:31.280 --> 00:19:34.550
And that's why they start stuff and
stop stuff and start stuff and stop.

00:19:34.550 --> 00:19:39.920
So my biggest criticism of Intel is
that we always quit stuff right before

00:19:39.920 --> 00:19:42.530
we could have made it, you know?

00:19:42.530 --> 00:19:44.280
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
&gt;&gt; You look at a company like Mellanox,

00:19:44.280 --> 00:19:48.000
a company you cannot even afford to buy.

00:19:48.000 --> 00:19:49.260
Many of you probably don't know this.

00:19:49.260 --> 00:19:54.110
It was a company that was ex Intel people
who took technology that Intel quit on.

00:19:55.750 --> 00:19:56.340
Right?

00:19:56.340 --> 00:19:56.850
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:19:56.850 --> 00:19:58.710
&gt;&gt; How are they doing that now?

00:19:58.710 --> 00:19:59.700
&gt;&gt; They're doing pretty good.

00:19:59.700 --> 00:20:01.430
They're doing just fine thanks.

00:20:01.430 --> 00:20:02.580
I mean there's tons of them.

00:20:02.580 --> 00:20:04.600
I mean they're all they're
littered throughout tech.

00:20:04.600 --> 00:20:06.720
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
&gt;&gt; Things that we gave up on.

00:20:06.720 --> 00:20:07.800
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
&gt;&gt; Even data center.

00:20:07.800 --> 00:20:08.300
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:20:09.510 --> 00:20:14.260
What I hear a lot of in that
response is that you really knew

00:20:14.260 --> 00:20:19.270
Intel you had a 28-year career there,
you had gone through different divisions.

00:20:19.270 --> 00:20:21.960
You were sort of a subject
matter expert on Intel and

00:20:21.960 --> 00:20:24.840
its tolerance for
risk-taking among many other things.

00:20:24.840 --> 00:20:26.430
&gt;&gt; And its personality in the company.

00:20:26.430 --> 00:20:28.450
&gt;&gt; Right.
And its personality and its culture.

00:20:30.260 --> 00:20:34.910
Do you feel any insecurities about
stepping into a new role where

00:20:34.910 --> 00:20:36.590
you won't have 28 years
with the company and

00:20:36.590 --> 00:20:40.310
you won't be a subject matter
expert in that respect.

00:20:41.390 --> 00:20:44.012
&gt;&gt; You know, I think I did for
a long time.

00:20:44.012 --> 00:20:46.171
I don't now, but I did.

00:20:46.171 --> 00:20:49.040
And I think that kept me from
ever leaving for a long time.

00:20:49.040 --> 00:20:52.780
I think that's why people
stay at companies so long.

00:20:52.780 --> 00:20:55.980
They love the work,
they know the situation well.

00:20:55.980 --> 00:20:59.370
And all the things they don't like
they know what they're about, right?

00:20:59.370 --> 00:20:59.960
I dont.

00:20:59.960 --> 00:21:05.280
I don't have that fear anymore
because I think one of the things that

00:21:07.290 --> 00:21:10.330
if you step back from the day to day,

00:21:10.330 --> 00:21:13.726
either there's a lot of pattern
recognition in the behaviors of companies.

00:21:13.726 --> 00:21:19.753
Different sizes and irrespective of the
content, a lot of the same challenges and

00:21:19.753 --> 00:21:24.121
opportunities exist about
consolidations in the market,

00:21:24.121 --> 00:21:28.330
about whatever's going
on in the competitive.

00:21:28.330 --> 00:21:32.391
And you can actually apply, I mean working
at semiconductors has nothing to do with

00:21:32.391 --> 00:21:36.568
banking, and I've been at the bank for the
last couple weeks being indoctrinated and

00:21:36.568 --> 00:21:40.490
I realized, wow pattern recognition,
there are a lot of similarities.

00:21:40.490 --> 00:21:44.260
When you have 200,000 employees working
in a whole bunch of geographies,

00:21:44.260 --> 00:21:48.760
having a whole bunch of challenges,
you have a regulatory regime that

00:21:48.760 --> 00:21:53.712
overlays the competitive environment,
you're like, wow, I get this.

00:21:53.712 --> 00:21:59.430
I understand some of the, and
then actually it's been really exciting.

00:21:59.430 --> 00:22:01.590
Like wow I could do something
completely different.

00:22:02.830 --> 00:22:04.400
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
&gt;&gt; Although I think banking's actually

00:22:04.400 --> 00:22:05.375
a tech business now.

00:22:05.375 --> 00:22:08.130
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
Well.

00:22:08.130 --> 00:22:09.210
You heard it here first.

00:22:09.210 --> 00:22:11.110
&gt;&gt; No I mean everybody knows that right.

00:22:11.110 --> 00:22:14.500
I mean the next billion people aren't
going to go open bank accounts.

00:22:14.500 --> 00:22:15.000
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:22:16.020 --> 00:22:17.870
&gt;&gt; It's a tech business.

00:22:17.870 --> 00:22:18.970
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:22:18.970 --> 00:22:21.830
And so
that makes it more appealing to you?

00:22:21.830 --> 00:22:23.010
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:22:23.010 --> 00:22:25.630
I love it.
I think it's fascinating.

00:22:25.630 --> 00:22:29.210
I think that entire way
that economies work and

00:22:29.210 --> 00:22:33.260
the movement of money and
the concept of crypto-currencies.

00:22:33.260 --> 00:22:37.390
And I think fintech and
what's going to happen in

00:22:38.390 --> 00:22:42.390
digital economies is going to be one
of the most interesting things that

00:22:42.390 --> 00:22:46.270
are going to happen in the next ten years,
and that everything we've been doing.

00:22:46.270 --> 00:22:49.330
One of the things that I realized,
I've been realizing is I've

00:22:49.330 --> 00:22:53.180
been on this journey to what comes next is
that everything I've been working on for

00:22:53.180 --> 00:22:56.330
the last 30 years is
actually infrastructure.

00:22:56.330 --> 00:22:59.980
Like everyone had to have computers to
be able to do any of this other stuff.

00:22:59.980 --> 00:23:03.510
We had to actually be able to
build computers at a cost that

00:23:03.510 --> 00:23:05.490
allowed them to be pervasive.

00:23:05.490 --> 00:23:08.650
And then all this,
I mean I can not begin to tell you,

00:23:08.650 --> 00:23:12.760
unless you work in semiconductors how
complicated it is to build semiconductors.

00:23:12.760 --> 00:23:18.770
It is so complex I mean it's mind
blowingly complex and dangerous.

00:23:18.770 --> 00:23:21.430
And we build these huge
chemical buildings, and

00:23:21.430 --> 00:23:24.620
they cost us 7 billion dollars, and
then we make these things that are so

00:23:24.620 --> 00:23:27.560
small no one can even see them, and
they want to pay us 20 bucks for them.

00:23:27.560 --> 00:23:28.670
That kind of sucks.

00:23:28.670 --> 00:23:30.840
And at the end of the day, it really does.

00:23:30.840 --> 00:23:32.170
It's a hard business.

00:23:32.170 --> 00:23:36.780
It is building the framework for
everything that's built on top of it.

00:23:36.780 --> 00:23:41.390
And everything that's built on top of
it now, computing is so powerful and so

00:23:41.390 --> 00:23:42.030
pervasive.

00:23:42.030 --> 00:23:42.950
And mobile is so

00:23:42.950 --> 00:23:47.760
amazing, you can do like, I mean,
you do not need to go in a bank anymore.

00:23:49.600 --> 00:23:52.930
And I think security and
identities, the identity thing,

00:23:52.930 --> 00:23:55.001
I'm just super excited about that.

00:23:55.001 --> 00:24:00.272
There's so much green space of what's
going to happen in identity management.

00:24:00.272 --> 00:24:03.499
&gt;&gt; You mentioned wanting to
do something that matters.

00:24:03.499 --> 00:24:04.335
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:24:04.335 --> 00:24:09.812
One of our application questions that we
all have to answer when we come here is,

00:24:09.812 --> 00:24:12.042
what matters most to us and why.

00:24:12.042 --> 00:24:18.560
What matters to you, what falls into
the category of something that matters?

00:24:18.560 --> 00:24:23.343
&gt;&gt; What matters to me is making
the world a safer place.

00:24:23.343 --> 00:24:27.297
So the things that I find myself
working on, my free jobs,

00:24:27.297 --> 00:24:31.427
my volunteer jobs [LAUGH] are for
the government and in cyber.

00:24:31.427 --> 00:24:36.586
And I think the,
even at Citigroup I started working

00:24:36.586 --> 00:24:42.200
on their anti-money laundering and
their cyber team.

00:24:42.200 --> 00:24:48.360
I think the ability to know what's safe
and good is going to become even more and

00:24:48.360 --> 00:24:54.941
more important to people in a world
that's less physical, right?

00:24:54.941 --> 00:24:57.950
And I think that's a very
important frontier

00:24:57.950 --> 00:25:01.870
because we have not made a lot of
not fundamental breakthroughs.

00:25:01.870 --> 00:25:04.290
We're really good in detection and repair.

00:25:04.290 --> 00:25:07.280
We're not really good in prevention,
and we're not really,

00:25:07.280 --> 00:25:09.000
really good in identity management.

00:25:09.000 --> 00:25:09.756
So, known good.

00:25:09.756 --> 00:25:10.940
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:25:10.940 --> 00:25:11.440
&gt;&gt; Right?

00:25:12.880 --> 00:25:14.690
&gt;&gt; How do you think
about that in terms of,

00:25:14.690 --> 00:25:16.580
you said those have been volunteer roles.

00:25:16.580 --> 00:25:17.647
&gt;&gt; They have.

00:25:17.647 --> 00:25:21.010
&gt;&gt; And you were appointed by
President Obama to help out with some of

00:25:21.010 --> 00:25:22.951
the critical infrastructure work.

00:25:22.951 --> 00:25:28.450
How do you make the decision between what
is going to be sort of a side service

00:25:28.450 --> 00:25:33.908
project, and what's going to be one
of the main tenants of your career?

00:25:33.908 --> 00:25:36.700
&gt;&gt; Well, I used to think
people would always say this,

00:25:36.700 --> 00:25:38.032
they say this all the time.

00:25:38.032 --> 00:25:40.584
You should follow your passion, the money
will fall and I was like that's just crap,

00:25:40.584 --> 00:25:41.584
that doesn't really happened.

00:25:41.584 --> 00:25:45.180
But it turns out it might be right,
I mean maybe I was wrong by that.

00:25:45.180 --> 00:25:49.210
Because I keep drifting towards the same
kind of projects and even as I got on to

00:25:49.210 --> 00:25:53.276
the board of Citi, that was the first
thing that I started to get involved in.

00:25:53.276 --> 00:25:55.496
So, I think that that's my passion.

00:25:55.496 --> 00:25:59.651
So I think it's going to'
become the primary, right?

00:25:59.651 --> 00:26:04.490
Because I really think it's important
work and it's not getting done.

00:26:04.490 --> 00:26:08.959
And there's a lot of small companies
doing bits and pieces of it.

00:26:08.959 --> 00:26:10.882
When the problem's in security and

00:26:10.882 --> 00:26:15.178
the problem I attempted to go after inside
of Intel, maybe that was a mistake and

00:26:15.178 --> 00:26:19.560
should of done it outside of Intel, is
to actually consolidate into a platform.

00:26:19.560 --> 00:26:21.956
There's no security platform.

00:26:21.956 --> 00:26:26.420
You know how, you guys are mostly,
well not everybody's tuned into those.

00:26:26.420 --> 00:26:28.210
But in the early days of computing right,

00:26:28.210 --> 00:26:31.160
there's all these little weird things,
different applications and

00:26:31.160 --> 00:26:34.600
then Microsoft, thank the Lord,
put it all together in a platform for us.

00:26:34.600 --> 00:26:36.270
And as much as we'd like to bitch and
complain about them,

00:26:36.270 --> 00:26:37.660
they changed the world.

00:26:37.660 --> 00:26:41.650
The same way that Google did for
application development in the cloud,

00:26:41.650 --> 00:26:44.430
right now Amazon's doing for services.

00:26:44.430 --> 00:26:46.480
So those platforms are quite important,

00:26:46.480 --> 00:26:50.770
because what they do is they move
the entire industry forward to a place

00:26:50.770 --> 00:26:52.640
where a whole bunch of
innovation comes on top of it.

00:26:52.640 --> 00:26:54.760
So that's what's happening in Amazon.

00:26:54.760 --> 00:26:57.100
Amazon is the new great infrastructure,

00:26:58.290 --> 00:27:01.900
kind of like Intel was in the 80s and 90s,
right, building the base infrastructure.

00:27:03.080 --> 00:27:07.210
So I think that security,
that hasn't happened.

00:27:07.210 --> 00:27:12.070
There's no galvanizing way to think about
how to put all this stuff together.

00:27:12.070 --> 00:27:13.810
There's different point solutions.

00:27:13.810 --> 00:27:16.800
So that's a piece of work
that needs to be done.

00:27:16.800 --> 00:27:21.051
&gt;&gt; So while you were at Intel, you were
the head of Intel's diversity initiative.

00:27:21.051 --> 00:27:24.832
The company pledged $300
million to recruiting and

00:27:24.832 --> 00:27:29.360
retaining more minorities and
women within the company.

00:27:29.360 --> 00:27:33.300
Was that something you drifted to or
was that something you sought out?

00:27:34.330 --> 00:27:35.460
&gt;&gt; That is a great question.

00:27:35.460 --> 00:27:40.845
The answer is I did not drift to it,
it reported to me and I embraced

00:27:40.845 --> 00:27:46.370
it wholeheartedly at a point
in my career where I thought

00:27:46.370 --> 00:27:51.710
that by me lending myself to it,
it would make it more viable.

00:27:53.050 --> 00:27:55.430
Up until the point that I was
the president of the company,

00:27:55.430 --> 00:27:59.360
I would say they had to beg
me to do women's initiatives.

00:27:59.360 --> 00:28:00.520
I hate them.

00:28:00.520 --> 00:28:03.370
I do, I think a bunch of women
talking to themselves about

00:28:04.380 --> 00:28:07.240
how things are does nothing.

00:28:07.240 --> 00:28:10.850
So I think one of the things
that was my big achievement,

00:28:10.850 --> 00:28:12.680
was to get a whole bunch of men involved.

00:28:12.680 --> 00:28:17.040
The he for she thing that's going on,
Intel has kind of the version of that.

00:28:17.040 --> 00:28:22.790
Which is internal, the men involved in
having group dialogue about diversity.

00:28:22.790 --> 00:28:26.660
And diversity is beyond women,
it's everyone

00:28:28.620 --> 00:28:30.850
and we weren't doing a good job with that.

00:28:30.850 --> 00:28:34.990
So I think we were able to do
a broader job in diversity.

00:28:34.990 --> 00:28:39.190
I was able to lend my
position authority to

00:28:39.190 --> 00:28:43.420
make it really get legs inside the company
and to some extent, outside the company.

00:28:43.420 --> 00:28:47.200
And so, I was excited and
passionate about that.

00:28:47.200 --> 00:28:50.760
I have a very strong sense of justice,
which is probably why I'm so

00:28:50.760 --> 00:28:56.179
attracted to the defense of cyber crime,
right?

00:28:56.179 --> 00:29:00.990
And I don't like that prior to this year,

00:29:00.990 --> 00:29:04.870
we would publish data that would say that
women didn't make as much as men now.

00:29:04.870 --> 00:29:08.140
That's been fixed, so
that's a fabulous accomplishment.

00:29:08.140 --> 00:29:09.860
We just released the data last week.

00:29:11.190 --> 00:29:14.530
But there's so much work to be done.

00:29:14.530 --> 00:29:17.190
I'm happy that we're having a dialogue,
I'm happy to see that there's a dialogue

00:29:17.190 --> 00:29:19.120
about it at Citi,
there's a dialog about it at Vodafone,

00:29:19.120 --> 00:29:22.010
there's a dialogue about it everywhere,
that's great.

00:29:22.010 --> 00:29:26.510
But it's a lot of work,
it's not done in one year.

00:29:27.580 --> 00:29:31.810
&gt;&gt; So, I'll give you another quote from,
this time, your mentor Andy Grove.

00:29:33.210 --> 00:29:39.290
He said, that kind of puts hair on
your chest, to do that and survive.

00:29:39.290 --> 00:29:43.610
As a woman in software, in a company
that's a manufacturing company,

00:29:43.610 --> 00:29:45.760
to do that, you have to have strength.

00:29:46.960 --> 00:29:50.838
As a woman at Intel,
what was it that put hair on your chest?

00:29:50.838 --> 00:29:51.605
&gt;&gt; Talk.

00:29:51.605 --> 00:29:55.705
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I'm trying to decide where to

00:29:55.705 --> 00:29:56.272
go with that.

00:29:56.272 --> 00:29:58.946
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So

00:29:58.946 --> 00:30:03.750
many think whoop,
showing up for work everyday.

00:30:03.750 --> 00:30:07.060
When I started Intel,
which was a complete fluke.

00:30:08.100 --> 00:30:12.000
I was working at a startup,
that Intel acquired.

00:30:12.000 --> 00:30:13.960
And my family,
I grew up in Silicon Valley.

00:30:13.960 --> 00:30:15.510
My dad worked at HP.

00:30:15.510 --> 00:30:17.470
All my friend's parents worked at Intel.

00:30:17.470 --> 00:30:20.250
I was like, yeah I am so
not working at that old man company.

00:30:20.250 --> 00:30:20.940
It is not happening.

00:30:22.150 --> 00:30:27.070
But then Intel bought us and then I was
like 22 years old and I had no money and

00:30:27.070 --> 00:30:28.130
I wanted to get my master's.

00:30:28.130 --> 00:30:32.290
So I'm like I'm going to have
to work there for a while.

00:30:32.290 --> 00:30:35.500
And I went into it with no idea,

00:30:35.500 --> 00:30:39.070
like I didn't apply to Intel and
want to work for Intel and

00:30:39.070 --> 00:30:44.110
build semiconductors, and I had no idea
what the semiconductor industry was like.

00:30:45.280 --> 00:30:47.645
So I was blissfully naive for
about ten years.

00:30:47.645 --> 00:30:51.161
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And then one day I got this review, and

00:30:51.161 --> 00:30:56.316
you know how employees aren't supposed to
talk about their pay with other employees,

00:30:56.316 --> 00:31:01.000
like that's against the rules at all
companies except they all do it, right?

00:31:01.000 --> 00:31:03.360
So then I found out that I made
way less than all the guys,

00:31:03.360 --> 00:31:05.410
and I'm like, wait a minute.

00:31:05.410 --> 00:31:07.540
Woah, wait a minute.

00:31:07.540 --> 00:31:09.910
So I went and asked my boss.

00:31:09.910 --> 00:31:14.130
He was a legendary guy who works in this
valley and I said, why am I being paid

00:31:14.130 --> 00:31:17.190
less, because I have the same amount of
experience and I've got a master's degree

00:31:17.190 --> 00:31:20.530
and they don't, and I manage more people
and my projects are really successful?

00:31:20.530 --> 00:31:23.510
And he's like, you probably make as
much money as you deserve to make.

00:31:25.450 --> 00:31:27.990
Holy shit, so what did I do?

00:31:27.990 --> 00:31:29.110
What do you do when that happens?

00:31:29.110 --> 00:31:30.260
You call your mother.

00:31:30.260 --> 00:31:33.480
So I called my mother and I said,
what do you think he's saying?

00:31:33.480 --> 00:31:34.403
She says, I think he's saying

00:31:34.403 --> 00:31:36.010
because you're a girl you
don't get to make as much.

00:31:36.010 --> 00:31:39.211
And I said, that sounds wrong,
my mother's like, it is, do something.

00:31:39.211 --> 00:31:42.845
You know my mother, my mother was the
first Santa Clara County sheriff, a woman.

00:31:42.845 --> 00:31:45.626
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So I called her and

00:31:45.626 --> 00:31:47.361
my gun toting sheriff mother.

00:31:47.361 --> 00:31:48.765
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And she says,

00:31:48.765 --> 00:31:52.598
this is an outcry,
you should cry AFLCIO and I'm like, God.

00:31:52.598 --> 00:31:53.992
[LAUGH] We're not unionized, Mom.

00:31:53.992 --> 00:31:57.283
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So I go to HR and I say,

00:31:57.283 --> 00:32:01.315
this is typical Renee I said, I think
there is something wrong with the data and

00:32:01.315 --> 00:32:04.326
they are like, no there is,
we can't show you that data.

00:32:04.326 --> 00:32:05.464
I'm like, but you have to.

00:32:05.464 --> 00:32:08.320
You have to prove to me that I
make the same amount as the men.

00:32:08.320 --> 00:32:15.795
And see this was the beginning of
a journey where I fought the system.

00:32:15.795 --> 00:32:17.085
I fought the whole time,

00:32:17.085 --> 00:32:20.410
and I didn't realize I fought
the whole time till the day I quit.

00:32:21.460 --> 00:32:24.141
And I was sitting there, and
I was thinking to myself.

00:32:24.141 --> 00:32:25.891
God I'm tired.

00:32:25.891 --> 00:32:26.750
28 years.

00:32:26.750 --> 00:32:28.980
because I fought
the system the whole time.

00:32:28.980 --> 00:32:36.530
A system that was resurrected in an old
model that Bryan and I, and I think

00:32:36.530 --> 00:32:40.860
Bryan's doing an outstanding job here
has revolutionized the culture of Intel.

00:32:40.860 --> 00:32:42.510
I was working so hard on that.

00:32:42.510 --> 00:32:46.041
And I give him a lot of credit for
that because that was really hard.

00:32:46.041 --> 00:32:50.916
But my God, 28 years ago can you imagine?

00:32:50.916 --> 00:32:52.020
And I was the only one.

00:32:52.020 --> 00:32:53.240
So then I got into this habit.

00:32:53.240 --> 00:32:55.490
This came out of this dumb review.

00:32:55.490 --> 00:32:58.940
Every meeting for like a decade I'd write
in the corner how the men to one ratio.

00:32:58.940 --> 00:33:01.378
So it'd be like 28 to one.

00:33:01.378 --> 00:33:06.198
25 to one, so this went on for a really
long time and so I think what really made

00:33:06.198 --> 00:33:10.599
me the person I am today is I fought for
equality from the day I got there.

00:33:10.599 --> 00:33:14.087
And every single year, HR be like,
God, here she comes again.

00:33:14.087 --> 00:33:15.619
I make them show me the data.

00:33:15.619 --> 00:33:18.583
And if anyone inked ahead of me and
they didn't get as good of a review as me,

00:33:18.583 --> 00:33:19.290
that was it.

00:33:19.290 --> 00:33:20.540
That was it.

00:33:20.540 --> 00:33:21.637
I laid down the law.

00:33:21.637 --> 00:33:23.478
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] I'm, and

00:33:23.478 --> 00:33:25.979
I never got a promotion I did not ask for.

00:33:25.979 --> 00:33:27.070
&gt;&gt; Like mother, like daughter?

00:33:27.070 --> 00:33:29.090
&gt;&gt; Exactly.

00:33:29.090 --> 00:33:32.540
&gt;&gt; Well, so
you mentioned Bryan in that comment, and

00:33:32.540 --> 00:33:37.113
you guys were touted when you came on,
you as president, he as CEO,

00:33:37.113 --> 00:33:41.200
management team as having
complementary skill sets.

00:33:41.200 --> 00:33:44.824
But that was a first for
Intel, the dual team.

00:33:44.824 --> 00:33:45.650
Can you talk?

00:33:45.650 --> 00:33:48.220
&gt;&gt; Well, as the first to appoint
the dual team together but

00:33:48.220 --> 00:33:49.704
it wasn't the first dual team.

00:33:49.704 --> 00:33:51.569
&gt;&gt; Right, okay.

00:33:51.569 --> 00:33:53.635
Appoint them.
&gt;&gt; Because, well, yeah, that's right.

00:33:53.635 --> 00:33:54.805
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
&gt;&gt; Because well,

00:33:54.805 --> 00:33:59.220
I want to correct that because Gordon and
Andy were a team for 17, 18 years.

00:33:59.220 --> 00:34:03.110
Andy was president and CEO and
Gordon was the CEO and chairman.

00:34:03.110 --> 00:34:06.340
And then Andy and
Craig were In the same set.

00:34:06.340 --> 00:34:10.410
Paul did not have a partner for eight
years, there was an eight year gap, and

00:34:10.410 --> 00:34:12.910
then they appointed both of us
at the same time which was.

00:34:14.170 --> 00:34:16.970
&gt;&gt; So
how'd the board come to that decision, and

00:34:16.970 --> 00:34:24.320
what are some of the advantages and
disadvantages of the co-leadership team.

00:34:24.320 --> 00:34:26.290
&gt;&gt; Well, I'm a fan of co-leadership.

00:34:26.290 --> 00:34:29.733
I still am, and even though I decided
I wanted to go lead on my own,

00:34:29.733 --> 00:34:31.374
I'm a fan of co-leadership.

00:34:31.374 --> 00:34:35.428
And I would have a co-leader.

00:34:35.428 --> 00:34:38.870
I think it works really well at Oracle,
it's working very well there.

00:34:38.870 --> 00:34:42.220
These jobs are too big for one person.

00:34:42.220 --> 00:34:46.780
When you have 200 plus thousand people,
I mean by city has a co-leadership.

00:34:46.780 --> 00:34:49.130
They have a CEO and they have a president.

00:34:49.130 --> 00:34:52.840
You just need someone to be doing
some of the other stuff, right?

00:34:52.840 --> 00:34:54.790
I mean, these are just big jobs.

00:34:54.790 --> 00:34:57.398
So I'm a fan of that.

00:34:57.398 --> 00:35:04.186
I think that when you have complementary
skills, that's fabulous, right, and I do.

00:35:04.186 --> 00:35:07.543
I think the complexity between Brian and

00:35:07.543 --> 00:35:13.661
I was that I have always been a strategist
and a market facing externally,

00:35:13.661 --> 00:35:18.611
doing partnerships,
building business, running PNLs.

00:35:18.611 --> 00:35:21.587
And Brian had been very
internally facing as COO, and

00:35:21.587 --> 00:35:24.200
we really were in role reversal.

00:35:24.200 --> 00:35:28.600
And I think that was one of
the more complex dynamics for

00:35:28.600 --> 00:35:33.240
us because Brian needed to learn how to
develop and lead a corporate strategy.

00:35:33.240 --> 00:35:37.080
And, that's an uncomfortable thing for
the CEO to have the president

00:35:37.080 --> 00:35:41.000
be more adept at that which is
the primary job of the CEO.

00:35:41.000 --> 00:35:45.380
I do think, to be fair to Brian,
he was extraordinarily,

00:35:48.400 --> 00:35:51.430
gracious and letting me settle out
of the strategy and do things.

00:35:51.430 --> 00:35:53.750
But a certain point in his tenure
he needs to run the company,

00:35:53.750 --> 00:35:56.380
he is the CEO and
that's the decision the board made.

00:35:56.380 --> 00:36:00.840
That was the point at which I said look,
this is your job Brian,

00:36:00.840 --> 00:36:04.650
you're the one who's going to die or
live on the decisions.

00:36:04.650 --> 00:36:07.397
If you want to go this direction go but
I don't agree.

00:36:07.397 --> 00:36:08.280
But you get to decide.

00:36:08.280 --> 00:36:11.100
You're CEO, so
I shouldn't work here anymore.

00:36:11.100 --> 00:36:14.170
And as painful as that conversation
was it was the right one and

00:36:14.170 --> 00:36:17.090
I think very adult, right?

00:36:17.090 --> 00:36:18.710
because he needed to set the strategy.

00:36:18.710 --> 00:36:20.703
It was time for him to do that and

00:36:20.703 --> 00:36:24.314
I that was the complexity of
our particular situation.

00:36:24.314 --> 00:36:29.509
Is that I was more skilled
to be a CEO in a second.

00:36:29.509 --> 00:36:34.733
&gt;&gt; Is that a conversation you wish you
had had earlier in your partnership?

00:36:34.733 --> 00:36:37.350
&gt;&gt; No, I actually think it went
exactly as it should of gone.

00:36:37.350 --> 00:36:42.514
Because I think at the point we were at,
Intel had been led by an external person.

00:36:42.514 --> 00:36:45.556
For the first time in history
the company would have hit sudden death.

00:36:45.556 --> 00:36:50.997
Company was a huge inflection, there was a
lot of unrest about what direction to do,

00:36:50.997 --> 00:36:55.673
we had to do a lot of changes that
aren't really apparent to you guys, but

00:36:55.673 --> 00:36:58.969
we had to do a lot of cultural
changes around comp and

00:36:58.969 --> 00:37:04.320
benefits and just a lot of stuff that
hadn't been dealt with for a while.

00:37:04.320 --> 00:37:07.973
So, I think the right thing for
the company was stability.

00:37:07.973 --> 00:37:12.060
I think the right thing for
the company was continuity.

00:37:12.060 --> 00:37:17.111
And Brian's brought a lot of change and
he's on his path with

00:37:17.111 --> 00:37:22.074
what he wants to do with IOT,
and I think that's fabulous.

00:37:22.074 --> 00:37:25.000
So I think we have time now for questions.

00:37:25.000 --> 00:37:28.437
We'll start with one question from Twitter
and then we'll head out to the audience.

00:37:28.437 --> 00:37:29.388
So please.
&gt;&gt; Twitter.

00:37:29.388 --> 00:37:32.295
&gt;&gt; Please be sure to raise your
hands if you have a question, and

00:37:32.295 --> 00:37:34.298
they'll find you up and down the aisles.

00:37:34.298 --> 00:37:35.228
&gt;&gt; So thank you both for

00:37:35.228 --> 00:37:38.680
wonderful insights and
great banter which I always a fan of.

00:37:38.680 --> 00:37:41.270
So the first question that comes
through is asking you know

00:37:41.270 --> 00:37:43.330
you've been in Silicone Valley
your whole life.

00:37:43.330 --> 00:37:45.140
If you could change one thing about it,

00:37:45.140 --> 00:37:48.740
be its culture, attitude, interest,
whatever what would it be?

00:37:49.770 --> 00:37:50.660
&gt;&gt; Wow.

00:37:50.660 --> 00:37:52.620
First of all I should say I love it here.

00:37:52.620 --> 00:37:53.790
I love the valley.

00:37:53.790 --> 00:37:58.650
There's not a lot I would
change except I would

00:37:58.650 --> 00:38:04.170
like it to be a little bit,
I'd like the diversity

00:38:04.170 --> 00:38:09.235
discussion to be more serious,
in the sense that we got more traction.

00:38:09.235 --> 00:38:14.575
It's one thing for big public companies to
do work on diversity, but the predominance

00:38:14.575 --> 00:38:18.765
of the employment around this valley
is not big, huge companies anymore.

00:38:18.765 --> 00:38:21.935
It's midcaps and entrepreneurial.

00:38:21.935 --> 00:38:23.445
People like to move around a lot, and

00:38:23.445 --> 00:38:27.280
I still think the diversity
issue plagues Silicone Valley.

00:38:27.280 --> 00:38:31.726
I cannot tell you how much
time I spent advising or

00:38:31.726 --> 00:38:39.113
spending time with really wonderful
companies still largely led and financed.

00:38:43.926 --> 00:38:46.550
&gt;&gt; Hi there, thanks so
much for taking my question.

00:38:46.550 --> 00:38:50.711
You mentioned briefly that you've never
received a promotion that you didn't

00:38:50.711 --> 00:38:51.215
ask for.

00:38:51.215 --> 00:38:53.690
Can you just elaborate on that a bit?

00:38:53.690 --> 00:38:59.105
&gt;&gt; Well, so this is fascinating and
it turns out, has been through,

00:38:59.105 --> 00:39:03.070
and I'm on a lot of
compensation committees, so

00:39:03.070 --> 00:39:08.313
in addition to Intel, right, and
see this over and over again.

00:39:08.313 --> 00:39:13.506
I am, as I admitted,
not the kind of big women's person.

00:39:13.506 --> 00:39:17.261
But I'm going to sound like I am
with these comments, but anyway.

00:39:17.261 --> 00:39:21.528
Women tend to not apply for jobs unless
they can hit every single criteria and

00:39:21.528 --> 00:39:23.540
they think they're perfect at it.

00:39:23.540 --> 00:39:25.903
So what happens is in interviews,
women will come in and

00:39:25.903 --> 00:39:28.767
they will tell us that 10% of
the things that they are not good at so

00:39:28.767 --> 00:39:30.970
therefore they're not good enough for
the job.

00:39:30.970 --> 00:39:34.730
Their male counterparts will come in and
they'll be 10% qualified of the job and

00:39:34.730 --> 00:39:36.479
they'll tell us all the reasons
we should hire them anyway.

00:39:37.550 --> 00:39:40.620
And this happens in every company
that I'm involved with and

00:39:40.620 --> 00:39:43.500
they're all big multinational companies.

00:39:43.500 --> 00:39:47.270
And so,
the same thing happens with promotions.

00:39:47.270 --> 00:39:51.764
I managed in a company of men,
I managed men.

00:39:51.764 --> 00:39:56.115
For 28 years, and some women, thankfully
but not that many, and they always ask for

00:39:56.115 --> 00:39:56.780
promotion.

00:39:56.780 --> 00:40:01.096
Every year at their review, you're not
going to get promoted every year, but

00:40:01.096 --> 00:40:02.656
every year I got asked for.

00:40:02.656 --> 00:40:05.164
And I never,
women never ask for promotion.

00:40:05.164 --> 00:40:08.484
Now I am an outlier,
I just behaved like my peers.

00:40:08.484 --> 00:40:11.955
All I did was behave like all
the guys that I worked with, and

00:40:11.955 --> 00:40:17.140
it worked out really great, but I don't
know what that is, it's a cultural thing.

00:40:17.140 --> 00:40:20.290
We see it everywhere,
it's in all of the diversity data.

00:40:20.290 --> 00:40:23.880
If you read anything online,
it'll tell you about this phenomena.

00:40:24.890 --> 00:40:28.350
So, I think one of the things you just
have to get comfortable with is that

00:40:28.350 --> 00:40:30.680
people ask to be promoted.

00:40:30.680 --> 00:40:34.190
They do, and then asking to be
promoted you get an idea about

00:40:34.190 --> 00:40:37.180
what their aspirations are, and you tell
them what they need to do to be promoted,

00:40:37.180 --> 00:40:40.840
and then if they actually do it, you're
better off as a leader in a company,

00:40:40.840 --> 00:40:44.680
because you got a whole bunch of output
and then you get to promote them.

00:40:44.680 --> 00:40:47.300
You don't get that same dialog usually so,

00:40:47.300 --> 00:40:49.940
I think that's the thing
that I always did.

00:40:49.940 --> 00:40:52.470
I'm sure I was super annoying.

00:40:52.470 --> 00:40:57.020
I'm really sure I was, but
&gt;&gt; Thank you Rene for taking my question.

00:40:57.020 --> 00:40:59.220
I would like to thank
you because I work for

00:40:59.220 --> 00:41:02.970
Soft Resolutions Group at
Intel under your leadership.

00:41:02.970 --> 00:41:05.480
&gt;&gt; Thank you.
&gt;&gt; I came through the diversity

00:41:05.480 --> 00:41:08.340
that I just loved Intel,
so thank you so much.

00:41:08.340 --> 00:41:12.900
My question is how many more decades
would the Moore's law still be true,

00:41:14.720 --> 00:41:16.070
how fast do you think it?

00:41:16.070 --> 00:41:21.530
&gt;&gt; Moore's law is well alive and
well and as I no longer work at Intel,

00:41:21.530 --> 00:41:25.590
I'm not in a position to comment
beyond that, but I will say that it

00:41:25.590 --> 00:41:29.260
is absolutely fine, that is the least
of the worries of things that.

00:41:29.260 --> 00:41:30.710
&gt;&gt; Not everyone know what Moore's law is.

00:41:30.710 --> 00:41:36.560
&gt;&gt; So, Moore's law is the ability
to create more performance

00:41:36.560 --> 00:41:40.920
every couple of years more in a,
in a smaller transistor package and, and

00:41:40.920 --> 00:41:43.920
or in a denser lithography, so

00:41:43.920 --> 00:41:49.150
people talk about going 28 nanometers
to 10 nanometers or this or that,

00:41:50.370 --> 00:41:55.850
it's very technical and exciting but the
fact is what it's done is it's allowed us

00:41:55.850 --> 00:42:01.620
to sequence the human genome
in a few days versus a year.

00:42:01.620 --> 00:42:05.570
And it's allowed us to
find unique treatments for

00:42:05.570 --> 00:42:08.530
cancer at the Knight Cancer Institute
in Oregon.

00:42:08.530 --> 00:42:12.080
As a result of being able
to build basically what it,

00:42:12.080 --> 00:42:16.250
ten years ago would have been a room full
of super computers on a single chip and

00:42:16.250 --> 00:42:19.150
that's the benefit of Moore's Law and

00:42:19.150 --> 00:42:24.760
it's what fuels the entire you know
kind of digital economy including

00:42:24.760 --> 00:42:29.730
self driving cars which require
massive amounts of compute power and

00:42:31.190 --> 00:42:35.280
some of the bigger breakthroughs, I mean
even in cyber and personal identity and

00:42:35.280 --> 00:42:39.580
there's a lot about algorithmic profiling,
so a lot of that requires just tremendous

00:42:39.580 --> 00:42:42.310
amount to compute that we
didn't have available to us and

00:42:42.310 --> 00:42:47.040
the Moore's Law has delivered that to us
in the last decade and will continue.

00:42:47.040 --> 00:42:51.620
And that's why I say Intel has been a
fundamental infrastructure company, right?

00:42:51.620 --> 00:42:56.115
because what they're doing is building
kind of the blocks upon which

00:42:56.115 --> 00:43:00.620
Tesla will make a self-driving car,
or Google, or whomever.

00:43:03.164 --> 00:43:07.750
&gt;&gt; Hello Rene,
as a former Intel employee working at LTD.

00:43:07.750 --> 00:43:08.587
Thanks for-
&gt;&gt; Look at all these adult people.

00:43:08.587 --> 00:43:11.719
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] Yeah, all these people.

00:43:11.719 --> 00:43:15.493
My question will be but
culture I see there in

00:43:15.493 --> 00:43:20.389
Oregon the Logic Technology
Development Department for

00:43:20.389 --> 00:43:24.371
a few years and
I found this hardware culture.

00:43:24.371 --> 00:43:29.065
You know about chip manufacturing
we value creativity but

00:43:29.065 --> 00:43:34.920
at same time we value more or
at least equally disciplined in execution.

00:43:34.920 --> 00:43:41.260
Do you find that culture is somehow,
separated from this

00:43:41.260 --> 00:43:46.800
more of a software culture like Google,
Facebook, Uber,

00:43:46.800 --> 00:43:52.820
we totally evaluate person our
individual individual creativity.

00:43:52.820 --> 00:43:58.850
Do you find that art is part
of Intel's current situation?

00:44:00.310 --> 00:44:05.440
&gt;&gt; So, I don't think that any company that
gets to the scale of Google doesn't value

00:44:05.440 --> 00:44:07.310
discipline in execution.

00:44:07.310 --> 00:44:11.530
I think, at a certain point in
the life cycle of any company,

00:44:11.530 --> 00:44:15.230
you get to the point where it doesn't
matter what you're doing, discipline and

00:44:15.230 --> 00:44:17.780
execution is critical to the results.

00:44:17.780 --> 00:44:23.260
And so, I think Intel is not unique,
I mean if you were building

00:44:23.260 --> 00:44:27.130
car batteries, you'd be in the same
situation as semiconductors, right?

00:44:27.130 --> 00:44:30.140
Very similar technology,
and that discipline and

00:44:30.140 --> 00:44:31.779
execution comes from health and safety.

00:44:33.080 --> 00:44:37.810
I find a very similar culture
discipline execution over content

00:44:37.810 --> 00:44:42.570
excitement about new innovation,
I'd say Vodafone.

00:44:42.570 --> 00:44:49.250
Because health and safety kind of
overrules sometimes innovation.

00:44:49.250 --> 00:44:53.770
So you get excited about something but
it might inject some kind of risk in to

00:44:53.770 --> 00:44:57.790
the health and safety, so when you build
things that people die building, right?

00:44:57.790 --> 00:44:59.980
Which when you are putting
up a mobile tower,

00:44:59.980 --> 00:45:04.660
people fall off them unfortunately or
they electrocute themselves.

00:45:04.660 --> 00:45:08.740
So when you're in that kind of a business,
health and safety tends to

00:45:08.740 --> 00:45:13.420
put a process regime or if you're in
a highly regulated business like the bank.

00:45:13.420 --> 00:45:18.740
It puts a process regime over innovation
and I think that's a difficulty and

00:45:18.740 --> 00:45:23.280
I think as a leader you have to
actually create a avenue for

00:45:23.280 --> 00:45:27.110
innovation, that's why you see so many
big companies creating these innovations.

00:45:27.110 --> 00:45:30.350
And you kind of wonder like if innovation
on this side doesn't really help, so

00:45:30.350 --> 00:45:33.290
it's complicated.

00:45:33.290 --> 00:45:35.720
You have to be able to have a process
where the innovation occurs and

00:45:35.720 --> 00:45:39.400
you pull it back in, right?,

00:45:39.400 --> 00:45:45.120
because this companies you get to
certain point and you just have process.

00:45:45.120 --> 00:45:47.388
It's unfortunate, that is part of scale.

00:45:50.895 --> 00:45:51.695
&gt;&gt; Hi, Rene.

00:45:51.695 --> 00:45:52.867
Thanks for joining us.

00:45:52.867 --> 00:45:57.162
You spoke about the importance of
having good mentors in your career.

00:45:57.162 --> 00:46:01.770
For many of us, we'll be going out to
the real world, again, and then hopefully-

00:46:01.770 --> 00:46:03.626
&gt;&gt; I'll be with you.

00:46:03.626 --> 00:46:06.245
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Yes, so I'd like to hear from you,

00:46:06.245 --> 00:46:10.365
best practices on finding
those good mentors and

00:46:10.365 --> 00:46:12.995
setting the stage such that
they want to invest in you.

00:46:14.330 --> 00:46:17.180
&gt;&gt; I think a mentorship
is a two-way street.

00:46:17.180 --> 00:46:20.720
So, I think finding a great mentor
isn't so much about finding the person,

00:46:20.720 --> 00:46:25.680
it's also what can you add
to that person as well.

00:46:25.680 --> 00:46:29.290
And so, for a great mentor,
or I'll give you an example.

00:46:29.290 --> 00:46:34.460
One of the people that is
in my Little Mentor circle

00:46:34.460 --> 00:46:40.730
is this wonderful guy who works at Google,
but he is bidirectional mentorship right?

00:46:40.730 --> 00:46:45.230
Like he's like you're I am
like LinkedIn seriously?

00:46:45.230 --> 00:46:46.390
Like I have a LinkedIn site.

00:46:46.390 --> 00:46:49.060
He's like your LinkedIn
sucks I'm redoing it.

00:46:49.060 --> 00:46:53.340
So I mean so it's like bidirectional, I'm
coaching him about you know from a career

00:46:53.340 --> 00:46:55.990
evolution perspective and
he's moving into management.

00:46:55.990 --> 00:46:58.080
He's never been a big manager,
that kind of thing.

00:46:58.080 --> 00:47:01.100
So I think it has to be bidirectional,
so you have to find someone that

00:47:01.100 --> 00:47:05.090
you not only can advise you, but
that you can actually help them either by

00:47:05.090 --> 00:47:08.990
bringing them forward in technology or
your interests or whatever.

00:47:08.990 --> 00:47:11.710
When they're not bi directional,
they're a bummer and it's really hard.

00:47:11.710 --> 00:47:14.050
Like the person never wants
to get time with you,

00:47:15.140 --> 00:47:18.830
you'll find they're like busy always and
it's like a burden, right?

00:47:18.830 --> 00:47:20.790
Like they want to say yes to you but

00:47:20.790 --> 00:47:22.390
it's like what are they getting out of it,
kind of thing.

00:47:24.150 --> 00:47:27.860
So also, if you're an entrepreneur, a lot
of people who may mentor you, sometimes

00:47:27.860 --> 00:47:32.810
would invest with you and then it get very
involve in helping you be successful.

00:47:32.810 --> 00:47:35.886
&gt;&gt; Well I'm going to
take the last question.

00:47:35.886 --> 00:47:37.636
&gt;&gt; We're done.

00:47:37.636 --> 00:47:41.970
&gt;&gt; And it has to do with your MBA.

00:47:41.970 --> 00:47:44.480
Sheryl Sandberg recently
put out an article

00:47:44.480 --> 00:47:48.150
saying that MBAs are not really
needed in the tech industry.

00:47:49.270 --> 00:47:52.550
I promise we will not take offense,
but do you agree?

00:47:52.550 --> 00:47:53.620
&gt;&gt; No.

00:47:53.620 --> 00:47:54.475
&gt;&gt; Okay, tell us why?

00:47:54.475 --> 00:47:57.977
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Well, not because I have one I mean, or

00:47:57.977 --> 00:48:00.964
that I love to go to the MBA
program here that I beg just for

00:48:00.964 --> 00:48:04.102
them to put the program together for
Intel people or that.

00:48:04.102 --> 00:48:10.284
I think, that education in general and
I think this undergraduate and

00:48:10.284 --> 00:48:16.210
graduate Teaches you not only
frameworks but tools to think.

00:48:16.210 --> 00:48:17.140
Right?

00:48:17.140 --> 00:48:21.360
And I think the MBA gives
you an opportunity for

00:48:21.360 --> 00:48:24.370
having had some experience in the world,
right?

00:48:24.370 --> 00:48:30.300
And being out there, to then come back and
get frameworks and, I talk

00:48:30.300 --> 00:48:34.250
a lot about pattern recognition, because
leadership is about pattern recognition.

00:48:34.250 --> 00:48:37.210
It's about seeing multiple
patterns occurring,

00:48:37.210 --> 00:48:40.010
understanding what that
competitive move might be, or

00:48:40.010 --> 00:48:43.860
recognizing that you've seen this
before and what happened the last time.

00:48:43.860 --> 00:48:45.140
And only through, I think,

00:48:45.140 --> 00:48:49.672
you know, understanding the frameworks
of competitive strategy,

00:48:49.672 --> 00:48:53.350
you know, economics, those kinds
of things, you need to know that.

00:48:53.350 --> 00:48:57.290
And by the way, CEOs should be able
to read a freaking balance sheet and

00:48:57.290 --> 00:48:59.310
an income statement and
know the difference.

00:48:59.310 --> 00:49:01.315
And if they can't, that's a problem.

00:49:01.315 --> 00:49:02.267
Really it is.

00:49:02.267 --> 00:49:05.626
And there are those that the first time
they ever have to deal with corporate

00:49:05.626 --> 00:49:09.160
finances when they become the CEO,
and you're like, really?

00:49:09.160 --> 00:49:11.680
because now it's complicated,
because the world is flat.

00:49:11.680 --> 00:49:15.880
And you have to deal with FX, and
you have to deal with hedging.

00:49:15.880 --> 00:49:17.850
And you might be like,
what are you talking about Renee?

00:49:17.850 --> 00:49:20.169
I'm going to run like a 100 million dollar
company, or a 10 million dollar company.

00:49:20.169 --> 00:49:21.446
Yeah, good luck with that.

00:49:21.446 --> 00:49:25.000
I mean, the economy, you're going to
sell to people all over the world.

00:49:25.000 --> 00:49:25.990
Guess what?
You're going to have to deal

00:49:25.990 --> 00:49:26.870
with tax and trade.

00:49:26.870 --> 00:49:29.620
because you have to deal with the tax
issues when you sell in all these places.

00:49:29.620 --> 00:49:31.850
An online tax, that's super complicated.

00:49:31.850 --> 00:49:33.200
How about safe harbor?

00:49:33.200 --> 00:49:36.810
So this is the stuff you learn
when you're in an MBA program.

00:49:36.810 --> 00:49:42.790
You learn about the kind of
environment of doing business and

00:49:42.790 --> 00:49:46.020
then you have contacts and
even if you don't know something,

00:49:46.020 --> 00:49:50.790
you know, I should probably call
a lawyer about that, you know, right?

00:49:50.790 --> 00:49:53.760
Or how to set up an entity
in a foreign place.

00:49:53.760 --> 00:49:55.632
So, I don't agree with that.

00:49:55.632 --> 00:50:00.318
I think we need, we have a whole
generation right now of super smart people

00:50:00.318 --> 00:50:05.456
who are at 10 year mark in their career,
10 to 12 year mark in their career,

00:50:05.456 --> 00:50:09.794
who want to be leaders, who have
absolutely no skills to be leaders.

00:50:09.794 --> 00:50:11.233
Zip!

00:50:11.233 --> 00:50:14.741
They should all come in the MBA program,
because they don't know anything.

00:50:14.741 --> 00:50:16.873
They all grow up in unicorns,
and they make tons of money, and

00:50:16.873 --> 00:50:19.298
they are like,
well I've done these like 5,000 projects.

00:50:19.298 --> 00:50:22.922
And you're like, that's great, but
you know nothing about all this other

00:50:22.922 --> 00:50:26.376
stuff because, see, the problem is,
once you get out of running a project or

00:50:26.376 --> 00:50:30.281
being in engineering or being in sales or
being in marketing, you have to actually

00:50:30.281 --> 00:50:33.479
then just do the boring stuff,
which is running the corporation.

00:50:33.479 --> 00:50:38.168
And that stuff is like a whole different
level of stuff, it's not that other stuff.

00:50:38.168 --> 00:50:41.086
It's the, gee, I wish people
wouldn't sue me for anti-trust.

00:50:41.086 --> 00:50:42.083
That really sucks.

00:50:42.083 --> 00:50:45.065
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Well it'd be good if you actually knew

00:50:45.065 --> 00:50:46.449
what anti-trust law was, right?

00:50:46.449 --> 00:50:49.978
So [LAUGH] and I taught it,
just at another business school,

00:50:49.978 --> 00:50:54.920
where some students didn't understand that
some of the things they were proposing

00:50:54.920 --> 00:50:56.278
were against the law.

00:50:56.278 --> 00:50:58.298
And then you learn that, right?

00:50:58.298 --> 00:50:59.778
You're in a business school class and
they say,

00:50:59.778 --> 00:51:02.246
that's great, except that there's
an anti-trust law against that, right?

00:51:02.246 --> 00:51:04.173
You can't combine with those people.

00:51:04.173 --> 00:51:07.122
So, Cheryl and I,
I guess we'll have to disagree on that.

00:51:07.122 --> 00:51:08.914
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:51:08.914 --> 00:51:10.718
&gt;&gt; If everyone would join me in thanking

00:51:10.718 --> 00:51:11.422
Renee James.

00:51:11.422 --> 00:51:13.185
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; Thank you.

00:51:13.185 --> 00:51:16.390
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; Thanks.

00:51:16.390 --> 00:51:21.670
[MUSIC]

